WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.060
A Bash script should make SSH key use explicit and reviewable.

2
00:00:05.160 --> 00:00:12.440
It should select the intended identity for the intended target rather than relying on whichever key happens to be available in the environment.

3
00:00:12.540 --> 00:00:19.100
This is especially important on developer workstations, shared runners, and deployment hosts where several keys may be present.

4
00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:23.440
The script should make its trust decision visible to reviewers.

5
00:00:23.720 --> 00:00:32.760
Quoting and option handling matter because SSH commands often combine paths, usernames, hostnames, options, and file names.

6
00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:37.980
Key paths and SSH options should be quoted where appropriate.

7
00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:43.360
Bash arrays can help preserve option boundaries when a script needs to construct a command from several pieces.

8
00:00:43.460 --> 00:00:50.900
Avoid building one large generated command string that mixes shell syntax with variable data and then re-parsing it.

9
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:54.680
Clear invocation is easier to review and safer to maintain.

10
00:00:54.960 --> 00:01:02.240
SSH agents can improve usability by avoiding repeated key prompts and by keeping private keys out of direct command-line handling.

11
00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:05.380
They also change the runtime exposure model.

12
00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:11.160
An agent can make keys available to child processes or sessions beyond the original intended use.

13
00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:16.220
Agent forwarding is especially sensitive because it extends agent access through another host.

14
00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:21.680
It should be avoided unless there is an explicit, approved reason and the risk is understood.

15
00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:27.640
When a required key is unavailable, a script should fail safely with a non-sensitive message.

16
00:01:27.740 --> 00:01:37.420
It should not try random identities until something works, dump agent contents into logs, print key material, or silently fall back to a broader key.

17
00:01:37.520 --> 00:01:49.200
Safe automation is predictable automation: the intended key is used for the intended target, and missing access is treated as a configuration problem rather than an excuse to broaden privilege.
